 | `src/voice-recorder.ts` | Batch capture: `VoiceRecorder` (spawns `ffmpeg` → WAV, graceful `q`-stop) + `transcribeAudio` (POST to `api.x.ai/v1/stt`) + `resolveWindowsAudioDevice` |
 | `src/voice-streamer.ts` / `src/remote-voice.ts` | Shared live STT: `PcmVoiceStreamer` accepts raw PCM16/16 kHz/mono bytes; local `VoiceStreamer` feeds it from ffmpeg and AFK Pilot feeds it from `media/pcm-worklet.js`. Stop waits for the `transcript.done` emitted after that stop's `audio.done` (earlier smart-turn finalizations do not satisfy it; 5-second best-transcript fallback), and browser capture waits for the worklet's ordered final-PCM/flush acknowledgement (500 ms bound) before teardown. Remote voice ownership is per client/tab (there is no repository-wide lease or fan-out); `RemotePcmIngress` buffers the bounded hands-free reconnect gap and owns duration/chunk/cumulative-byte limits. Before the host confirms STT is listening, `media/chat.js` retains up to 16 seconds / 512 KiB of PCM and flushes it in order; exhausting that bound cancels with a visible retry error instead of silently dropping the start. A `client-ready` resync cancels any host ingress for that rebuilt page before its snapshot is produced, so an uplink outage cannot restore host-only listening state. A detected send phrase returns a targeted `voiceSubmit` to the browser, whose normal `send` / busy-turn queue path crosses the relay for the same metering as typed input; the host never prompts ACP directly from STT. Remote audio is ephemeral and the xAI key stays host-side. **Dictation inserts where the caret is** (design from #72, Tarcisio Miranda, ported onto this architecture): `captureVoiceInsertion` snapshots the composer selection into `voiceBefore`/`voiceAfter` on start, so partials and the final transcript land there — or replace that selection — preserving the sentence suffix instead of always appending. The capture happens whenever a start is POSTED, deliberately NOT inside the `voiceConfigured` guard: the client is not the authority on the key, and a host that records anyway then rendered `"" + transcript` and wiped the draft. `renderVoiceInsertion` re-captures lazily as a belt, so anchoring-at-start is only observable once the caret moves between start and the first partial — which is exactly what its regression test does. A manual Send/Queue calls `stopVoiceForManualSend` (local: `voiceStop { discard:true }`; remote: the existing `stopBrowserMic` PCM path, never `SpeechRecognition`) and sets `voiceDiscarded` so an in-transit result cannot refill the composer the send just cleared; the host bumps a generation counter for the same reason. Spoken `grok send` deliberately keeps streaming |
