 clients get a top-left **← Back to app** link above search (Escape-equivalent, focus returns to the opener, inside the Tab trap); the VS Code tab does not. Remote keeps host-owned settings and connection status, the device-manager link, the live Grok inventory, and the grok.com link; Advanced host-local rows stay hidden. Tier-1 connector management uses the capability gate described above. Overlay is keyboard-contained (Tab trap + sibling `inert`/`aria-hidden`, focus restored after `paint`, Escape or Back to app returns to the opener). `update()` is a no-op when the displayed snapshot/env is unchanged (`paintKey`); a host-driven change while the phone category `<select>` is open (`describeChrome.navMenuOpen`) defers `paint` until that control blurs, so a live frame cannot close the native picker. Restore defaults only resets toggles/selects/sliders (`restoreTargets` / `restoreChanges` — never send phrase or dictionary terms); a page with nothing restorable has no button, and the action first expands an in-surface confirm listing the rows that will change. Snapshot-on-open for the tab; changes post the same `set*`/`open*` messages plus `setVoiceSendPhrase` / `setVoiceKeyterms` / `setTelemetryEnabled` / `setThumbsFeedback`. General includes *Thumbs feedback to SpaceXAI* (`grok.thumbsFeedback`, default off) as a desk toggle and a remote read-only status (same split as Anonymous usage stats — the phone cannot write a privacy setting the policy rejects). Grok.com connector rows show `scopeName` when the CLI sends a team name |
 | `media/chat.{js,css}` | Webview UI — chat rendering (markdown, LaTeX via MathJax, Mermaid), inline generated media, math/diagram export, session Markdown export (`exportCurrentSession` — ⋯ overflow on surfaces that ship `#session-head-actions`; VS Code gear Session item when that slot is absent → desk `openText` + `filename`, desktop Save As / VS Code untitled tab, remote `<a download>`). A cold `historyReplay` renders the last `HISTORY_WINDOW_USER_TURNS` (80) counted user bubbles (`splitHistoryWindow` in `webview-helpers.js`) and prepends earlier turns on scroll-up (`loadEarlierHistory` / `hydrateHistoryChunkWithCounters`, never unloads the live tail; each prepend takes only the deferred plan/permission cards whose turns it renders — `partitionHistoryCards`; prepend restores the anchor line in `prependHistoryNodes`). Find calls `expandHistoryAll` before searching. Live one-at-a-time turns are not windowed. Desktop View-all / proposed diffs / Read rows open the in-app preview overlay when `capabilities.previewInApp` is present (`openPreviewOverlay` — a Read fetches the file via `readProjectFile` and numbers it on the same `.tdl` gutter as a diff, with the agent's range marked; Copy / Save As / Open in file panel), history popover. RTL content: `applyAutoDir` stamps `dir="auto"` on every markdown block element, `unicode-bidi: plaintext` on prose containers covers loose paragraphs (renderMarkdown emits them bare + `<br>`, never `<p>`), code/pre pinned LTR, composer + its highlight mirror `dir="auto"` — content direction only, the chrome never mirrors. Tool calls grouped per batch + summarized by ACP **kind** (`categorize`/`summarizeTools`/`titleKind` — "Explored N items"/"Edited N files", not "Ran N commands" for reads); narration **interleaves** with its tool groups (`addToToolGroup`). Codex-styled tool/thinking rows, one lucide category icon per row (`toolIconFor`). Thinking traces **hidden by default** (`grok.showThinking`, #26) behind a `thinking-hidden` body class, with a **"Thinking…"** stand-in + `ensureActivityIndicator` (always some live-turn progress affordance); the floating **scroll-to-bottom** pill and the opt-in **Previous prompt** circle (#150) in `.composer` (`state.stickToBottom` is a user-gesture bit — wheel/touch/scrollbar/keys — not a 40px distance check; `stickThresholdPx` scales the near-bottom slack with line height). `scrollToBottom` / `forceScrollToBottom` / the pin MutationObserver no-op while `state.replaying` (ACP `session/load` *and* in-memory buffer rebuilds — do not treat that flag as "ACP load" elsewhere); the closing `historyReplay` frame follows the pin once so a large restore is not ~N synchronous layouts. Send/Stop (`state.busy`) is **event-sourced per session** — `agentStart` sets it, `agentEnd`/`agentError`/`exit` clear it, `clearMessages` resets it — so a focus swap/replay lands on the true state; **typed text or a staged attachment never cancels** (#37, Claude-style): while busy, Enter *and* the button click queue the message (`queueFromComposer` posts `queueSend` with chips when `capabilities.queueSendChips` — the queue is **host-owned per session**, see `session.ts` / `queued-send.ts`); a composer holding only an explicit chip is send-intent (the ambient editor chip is not). The pending message renders as **ONE user block pinned to the end of the chat** (`renderQueuedBlocks` — italic, dashed border, clock tag, plus the queued chips; Edit restores text+chips via `clearQueuedSends { restore:true }`, Remove discards) rebuilt from `queuedSends` snapshots so it **survives focus switches**, the button face tracks the composer (text or attachment → queue-send arrow, empty → square Stop, locked startup → disabled spinner with no cancel), and clicking Stop hands queued text and chips back to the composer + posts `clearQueuedSends { restore:true }` before the cancel (a halt must not auto-fire queued sends). An older host without the capability is refused with a visible error rather than queueing text alone. **Find in conversation (#99)** lives in this file (`openFind`, `window.__grokFind`) + `media/chat.css` — do not add a new `media/*.js` (the relay vendors a fixed list). See § ACP surfaces |
 | `media/webview-helpers.js` | Pure webview helpers shared with tests — file-ref detection, relative-time, mic-button state machine, trailing send-phrase highlight, the `@`-mention composer half (`getMentionQuery` token detection / `applyMentionPick` pick rewrite — see `src/mention.ts`), subagent classifier (`isSubagentToolCall`/`subagentLabel`), failed-tool reason (`toolFailureText`), the restore parsers that peel `buildPrompt`'s shapes back into chips (`parseAttachmentContext`, `parseSelectionBlocks`, `parseImageTags` — each strips only the leading/trailing shapes we emit, so a literal match mid-message survives restore), session Markdown export (`exportSessionMarkdown` / `exportSessionFilename`; `replayedUserBubbleVerdict` + `truncateExportEvents` keep the log aligned with visible turns), chat pin slack (`shouldStickToBottom` / `stickThresholdPx`), `createPendingOverlay` (optimistic colour/rename: a local overlay the next frame for that entity clears), and `contextOverheadTokens` / `nextContextBreakdown` / `contextBreakdownIsCurrent` (legend remainder, bound to the session/info `used` it was measured with). Open-path window (#102): `flattenHistoryMessages` / `splitHistoryWindow` / `countHistoryReplayCounters` / `partitionHistoryCards` |
 | `src/git-status.ts` / `src/git-run.ts` | The Changes view's host half, split pure / impure like the rest of `src/`. `git-status.ts` spawns nothing: it reads `--porcelain=2 -z -uall` so wholly untracked directories expand to individual files, and parses that complete status (spaces in paths, the two-chunk rename record, unmerged entries, an unborn repo with no `branch.ab` line), folds numstat and `log --not --remotes` into one `GitStatusSnapshot`, and PLANS every write — `planGitOp` returns the exact argv together with the rendering that goes in the host log, so the two cannot drift. Nothing user-supplied becomes an argument without a validator here: a path must be one the snapshot the host JUST read is reporting as changed (`isKnownChangedPath` — `--` and a pathspec are not enough on their own), and a branch name goes through `isValidBranchName` (rejects `-f`, `--force`, `HEAD`, `a..b`, `a.lock`, leading `/`, whitespace, shell metacharacters). `git-run.ts` is the process seam and the only place `execFile` appears: `git -C <root>`, `windowsHide`, `GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0` + `GCM_INTERACTIVE=never` so a credential prompt can never hang a phone, `GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0` on reads so a status poll cannot fight the agent's own git, `LC_ALL=C` because the parsers read English, and a `GitRunGate` per repository root so two clients cannot interleave writes. `runGit` never rejects — a missing git comes back as an outcome, not an exception. **There is deliberately no `git fetch`**: it is a network call on a path a person is waiting on, so `behind` is reported as of whatever last fetched and the view says so. Both hosts re-plan from their OWN snapshot rather than trusting the client's, which is what makes `gitRun` safe to classify `full` in `remote-policy.ts` — a remote already holds `send: "propose"` and `permissionAnswer: "full"`, so it can already ask the agent to run any git command and approve it; a closed set of four re-planned operations adds strictly less. |
@@ -1179,7 +1183,7 @@ remote `retryProviderSession` can restart only an already-connected provider.
 - **Run progress (P2-10).** How each agent sends subagents and workflows, and why the cards look the way they do: [subagents-and-workflows.md](subagents-and-workflows.md). `parseRunProgressUpdate` normalizes workflow and goal frames. Workflows and subagents share one header (`makeDelegationHeader`: icon, kind, name, step dots for workflows only — past four, three dots around the current, failed or first unfinished step, or the last three when every step is finished (`windowWorkflowDots`) — status, time, and a chevron only when there is something to open). Each live workflow has one full card, closed by default. With `grok.pinLiveWorkflows` enabled (the default), the transcript retains a muted started trace and the card lives in the composer pin; otherwise the card stays where it started. The expanded card's pin toggle moves every live workflow together, preserving open state, focus and scroll position. Desk preferences use global config; phones and browsers keep their own localStorage value. A finished run becomes a collapsed report at its original transcript position. Cold replay anchors a card after its launch row when the Grok receipt supplies the matching `rawOutput.run_id`. The host reconciles unfinished buffered runs against CLI workflow state before replay and on a two-second poll (`src/workflow-state.ts`), even without a viewer. `refreshWorkflowCompletions` repairs every buffered frame for the run in place, cloning the newest terminal observation into each replay position and delivers an unbuffered `runProgress` with optional `replaceOnly: true` through the existing local/session senders. Clients update an existing card or parked history entry without appending outside their window; snapshots carry ordinary frames at their original positions. An absent host-buffer entry is not rediscovered. Older hosts omit the hint and retain ordinary rendering; older receivers ignore it, so they can still append an out-of-window live repair. `syncWorkflowPin` renders live workflows in a pin that is a CHILD of `.composer`, collapsed by default per run in memory. Child rather than sibling on purpose: `.scroll-bottom-btn` and `.prompt-prev-btn` are absolutely positioned against the composer's padding box, so a pin outside it is painted over by both. The collapsed card shows only the header; while a run is live in this view, two minutes without a frame turns a "running" status into "no update for 2 min". Expanding shows horizontal wrapping steps with trailing arrows, one row per agent (tokens when above zero), the pause message, then the result through the reply renderer with the conversation's copy control, a muted token total, and controls on the one live card. Steps, header dots and agent rows share 9px state markers: green for done, blue for active, grey for pending (dashed for unknown, slashed for stopped) and red for failed. Each agent row discloses its own observed activity only when there is detail to reveal. Valid JSON results without a recognized prose field render as fenced JSON. Receipt age never implies token activity, and no workflow indicator animates. Optional fields gate their affordances; `displayName` never falls back to a run id. A matching named workflow frame suppresses its generic tool marker; without that frame the tool remains visible. Lifecycle status controls Pause/Resume/Stop and completion independently of the current phase. Additive `workflowContent` preserves result/pause provenance; new clients omit ambiguous legacy `detail` rather than treating event diagnostics as output. Goal percentages remain completion-only. See `research/run-progress.md`.
 - **Subscription usage (#159).** The context popover shows an account-labelled list of subscription windows in both app purposes, alongside occupancy. Missing data has a deliberate text state and no bar. `refreshSubscriptionUsage` is a separate request from the existing occupancy-driven `refreshContextDetails`, so context redraws never cause billing reads. All four host-message registries include `subscriptionUsage`, with remote mirror/scope delivery; reconnects carry only the latest snapshot. Credential-context changes invalidate the cache and retire the old process binding. [Provider contract and cache boundaries](../research/subscription-usage.md).
 - **`_meta.usage` → billing breakdown (#53).** Nested inside the prompt result's `_meta` (and mirrored on the rail's `turn_completed.usage`), extracted by `extractPromptUsage`. `usage.*` is whole-prompt billing across `modelCalls`; flat token siblings are the last model call; `totalTokens` is context size. `costUsdTicks` is fixed-point USD (**10^10 ticks = $1**, per xAI's `UsageTotals` contract). Missing cost/cache fields stay absent and render no row. `addUsage` sums tokens and cost through the same total, gated by `usageIsRealMeasurement` so `/compact` cannot double-count stale usage. Each prompt lives only in the session-id-keyed `SessionMetaOverride.usageLog` at its replay-stable `afterUserMessage` coordinate (successful zero-inference turns use a coverage-only entry); live completion, restore, and rewind all derive the aggregate from that persisted ledger. `enforceCompleteSessionCost` removes aggregate cost unless those coordinates span the whole conversation, while per-turn cost remains visible. Rewind truncates the ledger before recomputing, so discarded turns subtract both tokens and cost exactly.
-- **Vision input (image paste/upload).** Ctrl+V in the composer, drag-drop, and the + picker attach raster images (png/jpe?g/gif/webp — SVG deliberately stays a *path* chip so grok can read/edit the source; 20MiB cap per the xAI docs) as inline `{type:"image", mimeType, data:<base64>}` content blocks in `session/prompt`, plus a `[Image #N]` tag line in the text block (`envelope\n\ntext\n\ntags` — text keeps position 0 so `/commands` still dispatch; disk imports carry the origin path in the tag, `[Image #1] (assets/x.png)`, so grok can act on the real file). **The CLI still advertises `promptCapabilities.image:false` but actually accepts the blocks** (verified 0.2.87; behavior over advertisement — no capability gate, the `vision-prompt` live test pins it instead; see `research/vision-input.md` + `research/vision-probe.cjs`). Pending images live in a session-NEUTRAL staging dir (`<globalStorage>/image-staging/` — a grok session dir gets deleted by the empty-session cleanup, which would kill a pending screenshot), are pre-read + validated at send (any unreadable image **blocks the send** with chips intact — a dangling tag sends the model hunting for an image it never got), and are unlinked after send / chip removal, with an age-gated sweep on activation for orphans. `[Image #N]` is stamped once at attach (`allocateImageIndex`, `chips.ts`; `Session.imageIndexHighWater`) and never rewritten — the composer label, the bubble chip and the tag all read that stored index. The high-water resets only when nothing is staged (composer empty and queue empty), so a plain send still starts at `#1` while `#2` stays `#2` after `#1` flushes or is removed; tags may be non-contiguous. Authored text is copied verbatim. The CLI resolves a reference against the images on THIS message (measured, `research/image-index-probe.cjs`); a number from an earlier turn matches nothing. The webview holds send while a paste's FileReader is in flight (`pendingPaste`) and keeps the text half of a mixed clipboard; chips are host-owned — `send` carries only the text.
+- **Vision input (image paste/upload).** Ctrl+V in the composer, drag-drop, and the + picker attach raster images (png/jpe?g/gif/webp — SVG deliberately stays a *path* chip so grok can read/edit the source; 20MiB cap per the xAI docs) as inline `{type:"image", mimeType, data:<base64>}` content blocks in `session/prompt`, plus a `[Image #N]` tag line in the text block (`envelope\n\ntext\n\ntags` — text keeps position 0 so `/commands` still dispatch; disk imports carry the origin path in the tag, `[Image #1] (assets/x.png)`, so grok can act on the real file). **The CLI advertised `promptCapabilities.image:false` through 1.0.62 while it accepted the blocks** (verified 0.2.87; 1.0.70 advertises `true`; behavior over advertisement — no capability gate, the `vision-prompt` live test pins it instead; see `research/vision-input.md` + `research/vision-probe.cjs`). Pending images live in a session-NEUTRAL staging dir (`<globalStorage>/image-staging/` — a grok session dir gets deleted by the empty-session cleanup, which would kill a pending screenshot), are pre-read + validated at send (any unreadable image **blocks the send** with chips intact — a dangling tag sends the model hunting for an image it never got), and are unlinked after send / chip removal, with an age-gated sweep on activation for orphans. `[Image #N]` is stamped once at attach (`allocateImageIndex`, `chips.ts`; `Session.imageIndexHighWater`) and never rewritten — the composer label, the bubble chip and the tag all read that stored index. The high-water resets only when nothing is staged (composer empty and queue empty), so a plain send still starts at `#1` while `#2` stays `#2` after `#1` flushes or is removed; tags may be non-contiguous. Authored text is copied verbatim. The CLI resolves a reference against the images on THIS message (measured, `research/image-index-probe.cjs`); a number from an earlier turn matches nothing. The webview holds send while a paste's FileReader is in flight (`pendingPaste`) and keeps the text half of a mixed clipboard; chips are host-owned — `send` carries only the text.
 - **Remote document upload.** `initialState.capabilities.uploadFile === true` is the additive host capability proof; an updated web portal may then send `{type:"uploadFile",name,data}` with base64 bytes. The host re-validates the sanitized basename, six-extension allowlist, and shared 20MiB cap, stages to `<globalStorage>/file-staging/<uuid>/<basename>`, and routes the path through `addDroppedFile`, producing the same explicit path chip and `Attached file:` prompt as local drag-and-drop. Sent documents are retained in `SessionMetaOverride.uploadedFiles` until the last referencing session/fork is deleted because Grok reads the path lazily; only unreferenced orphan directories are swept after seven days.
 - **Command details (#41).** The extension runs every shell command itself (the mandatory `terminal/*` handlers), so each command row carries an always-available, collapsed-by-default expandable detail — a Claude-Code-style **IN/OUT block** on the shared code-chip surface: IN = the FULL command text from `rawInput.command` (grok truncates its titles), OUT = the complete captured output — each **capped to the first `MAX_COMMAND_PREVIEW_LINES` (6) logical lines, no inner scroll** (#71; pure `commandTextPreview`), the rest reached via **"View all"**: VS Code posts `openText` (untitled tab; commands carry `initialState.commandLanguage` from `commandLanguageForDialect`, output omits language so the editor can detect; a real shell command — `rawInput.command`/`.cmd`, or an `execute` approval card — opens under a `# Command` first line (`REM Command` for cmd) because VS Code names an untitled tab after its first line (#175), while MCP arguments, Read paths and output open verbatim); desktop with `capabilities.previewInApp` opens the in-app overlay (`openPreviewOverlay` — highlighted via `GrokSyntaxHighlight`, Copy / Save As through filename-bearing `openText`; a Read fetches the whole file via `readProjectFile` and marks the agent's lines on the `.tdl` gutter, with **Open in file panel** when the path is in-workspace); an older desktop host that never sent the flag still opens the bare viewer window; on a remote client (no host editor) it **expands inline** (`IS_REMOTE`, the same audience-split the diagram export uses) — a single long logical line shows in full. Snapshotted at `terminal/release` in `acp.ts` (`commandDone` → `commandOutput` host message; the buffer is exactly what grok received — same byte cap — plus a 100K display cap via `capCommandOutput`). Every tool row is ONE line (`.tool-item-label` span, CSS-ellipsized at the row edge as backstop; `toolLabel`'s 40-char clamp is the deliberate scannable-summary trim — the full text lives in the detail; an unpredicted title over `TOOL_LABEL_MAX` (50) is `middleElide`d (both ends kept) and the label span's `title` is the full string; a COMMAND row instead reads "Run \<program\>" via the pure `commandProgramLabel` (webview-helpers.js): first token, path-stripped/de-quoted, + one non-flag **bare** subcommand (a *quoted* next token, or one containing a path separator, is an argument/banner not a subcommand, so it's dropped — `Write-Output '=== 1. git status ==='` → "Write-Output"; `node research/x.cjs` → "node"), first-statement-only, "command" fallback. A **`(cd dir ; cmd)` subshell** (grok's POSIX navigate-then-run idiom, emitted even against PowerShell) is unwrapped — the `( )` stripped and a leading `cd <dir>` prelude skipped — so it labels the real command (`(cd … ; node x)` → "node"), not "(cd"; the cd-skip is scoped to subshells, so a user-typed `cd src && npm test` still reads "cd src" — so `git status`/`npm test`/`Get-Date` stay scannable while a `node -e "…"` payload collapses to `node`); a lone RUNNING command's group is expandable immediately (`cmd-single` — header chevron shows mid-run and expanding auto-opens the row's IN/OUT; multi-tool groups keep their chevron until the batch closes); expandable command rows carry a TRAILING chevron (› collapsed ↔ v expanded, `expanded` class) + hover brightening (`has-details`, `wireCommandToggle` — plain rows like the output poller don't react), and clicks inside the block never collapse it (text selection). Exit codes merge into the output: exit 0 with output is silent, exit 0 with **no** output renders a muted `✓ done · no output` marker (no empty `(no output)` pre), non-zero renders an `[Error] exit N` marker + error-tinted output **and rolls the failure up to the row (`.tool-failed`) + group (`.has-error`)** — a failed command signals at a glance like a `status:"failed"` tool (`markToolFailed`), not just inside its OUT box, a live kill (`commandOutput.cancelled`, always stated by this host — true only from `commandDone` when `exitCode` is null; false on every other `commandOutput`, including session/load hydration) renders muted `[Cancelled]` — the webview keys that marker on the field when present (`commandOutputWasCancelled`), and on `exitCode == null` when an older host omitted it (those hosts never hydrated replay output); not `state.replaying`. Output attaches by **two paths, one per agent execution model**: **grok-build** delegates commands via `terminal/create`, so the client snapshots the buffer at release and attaches by exact command string (`commandOutput` → `state.pendingCommandDetails`, `attachCommandOutput` in chat.js) with a standalone fallback row so output is never dropped; the **cursor/Composer** agent runs commands in its own CLI-side persistent shell (never `terminal/create`), so its output rides the **completed `tool_call_update.rawOutput`** and is attached by **`toolCallId`** (`maybeAttachToolResultOutput` + the pure `extractToolResultOutput` in webview-helpers.js — unfenced string + the same 100K `capCommandOutput` as the host, so a buffer rebuild of a live Claude row still has OUT; order-independent, because Composer runs commands in parallel and finishes them out of issue order, so a FIFO/order-based guess would misattribute). Do **not** gate that attach on `state.replaying`: `historyReplay` also wraps in-memory rebuilds (`focusSession` / `rehydrateWebviewFromFocused`), and live Claude has no `commandOutput` in the buffer. Both paths reference the same `details` node stored on the `pendingCommandDetails` entry (keyed by both `command` and `toolCallId`), which survives a lone command's flatten to `.tool-flat` (the flatten MOVES the chevron + details nodes). `grok.expandCommandOutputs` (webview-only, default false; Settings → General → **Expand tool details** — label renamed to match the two Command-Palette commands, setting KEY unchanged; mirrors the showThinking pattern via `applyExpandCommandOutputs`) pre-opens every detail **and** every detail-bearing tool GROUP (a group is detail-bearing iff it has a `.has-details` row — a command's IN/OUT, a Read file's View all, or an edit's diff; search/list-only groups stay collapsed). **Read rows (#122)** reuse the same IN/OUT + `appendCommandPreview` View all chrome: IN is the path, OUT is `content[].content.text` (or `extractToolResultOutput`); the title adds `lines a-b` from `rawInput.offset`/`limit` (1-based; 0→1) or completed `FileContent.total_lines`, and invents no numbers when those are absent. — the group open state is applied both live (`applyExpandCommandOutputs`) and at group finalization (`closeToolGroup`) — except on a group the **user** has toggled (`el._userToggled`, set by the header click), which `closeToolGroup` leaves alone: expanding a *running* batch to watch it must not be undone the instant it finishes. An explicit setting change or Expand/Collapse-All latch flip still force-applies through `applyExpandCommandOutputs`, so a global action always wins. On-demand, the Command Palette `grok.expandAllToolDetails` / `grok.collapseAllToolDetails` (titled *…(This Session)*) → `sidebar.setAllToolDetails` **`emit`s** (not `post`s) a `setAllToolDetails` host message that flips a per-session **latch** — `state.toolExpandOverride` (tri-state `null | true | false`) in chat.js. The latch force-expands/collapses **every** group (incl. explore/edit) + IN/OUT box — including a still-running batch — and keeps applying to later tool calls, because all render points flow through the effective-expand helpers `groupShouldExpand`/`detailShouldExpand` (`override ?? persisted-setting`). It beats the persisted setting (last action wins; flipping the gear switch or the `expandCommandOutputs` message clears it), **rides the session replay buffer** (the `emit`, not a `post`) so it survives Agent-Dashboard focus-swaps, and resets on a cold reopen (`resetForNewSession`) — never persisted to disk. CSS shows a running group's chevron once it's `.expanded`. Failed non-shell tools surface their reason via `toolFailureText`, which mines variant-keyed `rawOutput` blobs (e.g. `list_dir` → `NotFound`, `read_file` → `FileReadError`) after `message`/`content`/`error`, else the generic "Tool call failed." Radius scale: bubble 12 → block code/IN-OUT 8 → inline chip 6 (`--chat-radius-*`). `session/load` does not re-run terminals. grok 1.0.5+ replays a completed execute `tool_call` whose `content` is raw stdout — never `rawOutput.output_for_prompt`, which prefixes `exit: N`. Claude replays two rows: a pending `tool_call` whose `content` is the tool description and whose `rawInput.command` (also `title`) is the command, then a completed `tool_call_update` whose `rawOutput` is the bare stdout string and whose `content` is that stdout wrapped in a markdown console fence — prefer the string, join the command by `toolCallId`, `exitCode` stays null. Codex `session/load` emits no tool rows (the `formatted_output` branch matches the live normalizer only). The host emits the same `commandOutput` (`commandOutputForToolCall`, replay-only, same 100K cap, `cancelled: false`). An unrecognized provider shape degrades to no OUT. **MCP calls reuse this shell:** `prepareMcpToolCall` (`src/mcp-tool.ts`) folds grok `search_tool` into the explore tool-group (`kind:"search"`) and drops Codex `mcp__<server>__startup` throughout its lifecycle (failed startups go to the host log, with the server name and forwarded text), reads provider metadata before argument-key heuristics, always stamps `detailInput` on recognized MCP rows (`null` while Claude args are pending, `{}` for a no-argument call), and emits `commandOutput` joined by `toolCallId` from the complete measured per-provider result (not ACP `content`; grok/codex omit it): Codex `result.content` text plus pretty-printed `structuredContent` (or `error` when non-null), grok `OkayOutput` and any sibling `output` variants, Claude a content-block array (non-text leaves a JSON trace) or a string `rawOutput` shown verbatim (the completed update only — not `_meta.claudeCode.toolResponse`). That MCP `commandOutput` always states `agentSawCut: false` so the webview does not claim the agent saw the 100K display cut; shell `commandOutput` states `agentSawCut: true`. An older host that omits the field is worded as `output truncated` with no attribution (`commandOutputTruncationNote`). Webview qualification is `rawInput.command` / `.cmd` / `detailInput`. Session export keeps MCP rows on the generic tool path — `detailInput` is not a shell command. Shapes: `research/mcp-shapes.md`.
 - **Edit diffs inline (#45).** Every `kind:"edit"` tool row carries an always-visible `+N −M` change count (green/red, rolled up onto the collapsed group header as **path-deduped** totals, e.g. *Edited 2 files · +7 −2*) **plus** an expandable inline diff on the same detail surface commands use — so an edit is auditable in chat even under **Auto-accept**, where no permission card fires. This is decoupled from the permission flow on purpose: the diff `content` block (`type:"diff"`, with `oldText`/`newText` = the replaced region) rides the `tool_call_update` regardless of permission mode, and whether a permission card even appears is grok-build/platform-dependent, not a config toggle (verified 0.2.99: default config sends 0 permission requests for in-workspace edits; `research/edit-diff.md`). The pure `computeLineDiff(oldText,newText)` (`webview-helpers.js` — LCS backtrack, CRLF-normalized for compare+display, empty region = 0 lines so a new file reads as pure adds) yields the `+/−` counts and the diff rows. **A block is expanded into one hunk per replaced SITE** (`extractDiffSites` in chat.js): the block's `oldText`/`newText` is the search *pattern*, so a `replace_all` block describes only the token and alone renders 148 occurrences as one meaningless `+1 −1` — `_meta.details[]` is the complete account (one entry per site, `{old_string, old_line, new_string, new_line, context_before, context_after, line_prefix}`, real 1-based **post-edit** file lines; verified 12/12 against ground truth, `research/edit-diff-lines.log`). Per delivery shape: **completed + session/load** carry `details[]` → a site each, rendered `line_prefix + old_string` → `line_prefix + new_string` (there is **no `line_suffix`** on the wire, so the line's tail is genuinely unavailable and is not reconstructed); the **echo** carries no `details[]`, only a first-site-only block `_meta.{old_line,new_line}` → one approximate hunk the completed update then upgrades; neither (a whole-file Write's `_meta:{}`) → one hunk seeded 1 via `fileLineOr1` (also the guard for absent/bogus — 0/negative/non-number). A creation (`old_string:""`) takes no prefix, so a new file stays pure adds instead of the prefix becoming a phantom del. `buildInlineDiffRegion(hunks)` renders them **Codex-style**: **ONE `.tool-diff-region` per BLOCK holding N hunks** (never one region per site — 148 sites would otherwise stack 148 boxes) of `.tdl` rows, each a `[+/− sign][line-number gutter][code]` grid with a colored left-border stripe + subtle per-line tint; each hunk seeds its own gutter, and a quiet `.tdl-sep` hairline parts **non-contiguous** hunks (never before the first, never between contiguous ones). the region shows the first `DIFF_PREVIEW_LINES` (12) rows with a **"Show more"/"Show less"** toggle that grows it **inline, no inner scroll** (#71; remote-safe, unlike the host-local `open diff →`); `MAX_INLINE_DIFF_LINES`=400 is the hard budget **across** a block's hunks (1000 sites must not paint 2000 rows), the `open diff →` remainder note hidden until expanded — but the `+N −M` is summed over **every** site *before* the cap, so a capped render still reports the true magnitude. Del shows the old-side number, add/context the new-side. The gutter track sizes per region to the widest number rendered (`--tdl-num-w` = `max(4ch, digits+1)`) — 4ch through 999, wider only for a 1000+ line file. The `+/−` glyph is a color-blind affordance beside the border. Colors are **Codex's exact palette** (`--tdiff-*` CSS vars, dark default + `body.vscode-light` override): add `#40c977`/`#00a240`, del `#fa423e`/`#ba2623`, backgrounds the same at 23%/15% alpha — reused for the `+N −M` stat too. `applyToolDiffs`/`attachDiffPreviewToToolItem` handle BOTH the live `tool_call_update` and the session-load completed `tool_call` replay shapes (#30); a single call can carry multiple `diff` blocks (rendered as an array), each getting exactly **one** `open diff →` (per-site would emit 148 buttons; its payload stays the block's own `oldText`/`newText`). **grok reports each edit's diff twice** — an optimistic pre-write echo, then the authoritative completed update — and for a whole-file Write that OVERWRITES a file the echo carries `oldText:""` while the completed one carries the real prior content, so the echo must not win: idempotency is keyed on `item._diffSig` (the serialized diffs, **sites included**, so the `details[]`-seeded render outranks the echo's approximate one), making an identical replay a no-op while a *different* later diff repaints the row (reusing the detail node, or `wireCommandToggle`'s listener would strand + double-bind). **Totals paint as each edit lands**, not at batch close — the data is on the wire ~2.2–3.3s before the turn ends (`research/edit-diff-timing-probe.cjs`). `recomputeGroupDiffTotals` *recomputes* from the rows' `_diffStat` (so a corrected row replaces rather than double-counts) and `paintGroupDiffTotals` owns a `.tool-group-diff-totals` span, re-painted after each of the two things that wipe the header: `addToToolGroup`'s per-call `hdr.innerHTML` rebuild and `closeToolGroup`'s label reset. Painting earlier still, from the initial `tool_call`'s `rawInput`, is deliberately NOT done: it buys ~30ms and reconstructs the same wrong `oldText:""` for a Write. None of this expands anything — totals ride the *collapsed* header; only `grok.expandCommandOutputs` / the latch open rows. The native `open diff →` link (→ `sidebar.ts` diff editor) stays on VS Code and older hosts, and is never drawn on a remote, where it posted a host-local message nothing could answer while the inline diff it would have opened was already on screen above it; desktop `previewInApp` routes it into the same overlay at full size (`requestDiffPreview`) instead of a bare window, with **Open in file panel** when the path is in-workspace. `summarizeTools` counts edits by unique `toolFilePath` so "Edited N files" matches the deduped header totals. **Turn-level file summary (#83, @datvm).** After (and during) an agent turn that mutates files, a **Changed N files · +A −R** card lists each path (case-insensitive path merge; multi-edit **sums** every edit's +/−) with click → a whole-turn diff where a baseline is available, otherwise the capability-scoped fallback described below; deletes via ACP `kind:delete` or shell `Remove-Item`/`rm`/`del` (`parseShellDeletePaths`) show as **Deleted**. Pure `aggregateTurnEdits`/`turnDiffSummaryTitle` (`webview-helpers.js`), filled from `attachDiffPreviewToToolItem` + `maybeRecordTurnDelete`, reset on `agentStart`/next user bubble. Non-delete shell mutations stay out of scope (`research/edit-diff.md` § Turn-level). `turnDiffSummaryEnabled()` depends only on Coding purpose. The header is a native button with a chevron and `aria-expanded`; it toggles that card's file list and Open Changes foot, preserving its state through live repaints. `grok.expandDiffCard` defaults false, independently of tool details and the session Expand/Collapse All latch. A live preference change applies to every loaded card through `applyExpandDiffCard`. Like `promptNav`, this preference is host-backed on desk (including the separate IDE Settings webview): `setExpandDiffCard` writes global config, the listener broadcasts `expandDiffCard`, and initial state carries the default. The remote stores its own choice in `grok.remote.expandDiffCard`, ignores the desk's initial-state value, and both protocol directions are `host-local`; old hosts that omit the field leave desk cards collapsed. Knowledge work hides cards with `hide-turn-diff-summary` without removing them. Both appends go through `appendTranscriptChild`, so a card lands in the history park during hydration and never inside a transcript mid-wipe; the park saves and restores the live turn's tracker across the replay. At each live prompt boundary, `startTurnDiffBaseline` starts `git stash create` behind `gitRunGate` without awaiting it; a clean tree uses the captured HEAD SHA. Capture failures, busy repositories, tool activity before capture completes, and late completions after a turn/session change leave no usable baseline. The host retains at most 100 turn identities, their repository-bound SHAs, and bounded maps of pre-existing untracked paths to loose blob IDs; `captureGitTurnBaseline` excludes ignored files, drops the entire map above the path-count cap (hundreds of untracked paths mean an unignored build directory, where an arbitrary subset buys nothing), and skips only the offending file above the per-file byte cap (one large artifact must not cost its small neighbours their diffs). The additive `turnDiffBaseline` frame stamps identity and cwd onto the card; `turnFileDiff` requests that identity and a path, never a ref. Its handler repeats `gitFileDiff`'s root fence, then compares a mapped untracked blob with the current file's blob regardless of current status. Other paths keep the live changed-path fence and the baseline-SHA or new-file diff path. `turnFileDiffResult` carries the capped patch to the requester. `parseUnifiedDiff` ? `patchRowsToDiffHunks` ? `buildInlineDiffRegion` renders every file's hunks together in its card on all chat surfaces. **What an absent baseline means depends on the HOST, so it is a capability, not a fallback.** `turnDiffBaselines: true` in `capabilities` (opt-in field presence, `sidebar.ts`) says this host captures them at all; a host that omits this capability keeps the original behaviour — the row stays a button revealing the tool call that made the edit, and the card says nothing. On a host that DOES keep them, an absent baseline means the map died with the window (it is in memory, filled at turn start, never persisted), and the reveal is withdrawn rather than offered: it opens the LAST edit of a file the turn may have edited three times, while the row's own `+/−` describes all three, so the row and the thing it opened disagreed and the counts were the honest half. Those rows become plain text and a `.turn-diff-summary-note` at the foot says the diffs are kept only while the session stays open. `revealToolDiff` is still wired for the third case — a baseline exists and the host's git capture failed. On a desk, `open diff` sends the host-local `turnFileOpenDiff` through the same baseline/root/path fences: it reads the complete before side from the captured blob or commit (empty for a turn-created file) and the current file via `readFileForDiff`, then opens a `Turn diff:` tab (`preview: false`) outside permission cleanup; Electron uses its host diff viewer because the in-app overlay only has the capped patch, and remotes offer no native control. Untracked content present at turn start uses blob-to-blob patches; newly created untracked files and turns whose untracked capture was skipped retain the whole-file addition preview. Blob IDs never cross the wire. Current file contents include the user's mid-turn edits. Row totals remain the wire-edit sums; the projects rail retains its separate renderer. Beneath the rows, an **Open Changes** link takes the card's question (*what did this turn touch*) to the next one (*what is uncommitted now*), which until then meant finding the panel and pressing a glyph. `filePanelController()` finds the mounted panel — deliberately not `previewFilePanelController`, which is gated on whether previews open in-app, a different question — and the link appears only when that panel answers `canShowChanges()`, so a knowledge-work session, a folder that is not a repository and a panel too old to know the method each get silence rather than a dead control. Capability detection on the method, never a version.
